---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import JsonToTypescript from '../../components/JsonToTypescript.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="JSON to TypeScript Interface" description="Paste a sample JSON object and generate matching TypeScript interfaces, including nested objects.">
  <JsonToTypescript client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why generating types beats writing them by hand</h2>
  <p>Manually writing TypeScript interfaces for a complex, deeply nested API response is tedious and error-prone — a generator infers the shape directly from real sample data, saving time and reducing the chance of a typo'd field name or wrong type.</p>

  <h2>How to use this tool</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste a representative sample JSON object (with realistic values).</li>
    <li>Copy the generated TypeScript interface(s), including nested types.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A JSON object like <code>{`{"user":{"name":"Alice","age":30}}`}</code> generates a root interface with a nested <code>User</code> interface for the inner object, properly typed.</p>

  <h2>Common JSON to TypeScript Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Using an unrepresentative sample</strong> — if your sample JSON has a null value where the field is sometimes a string, the generated type won't capture that variability.</li>
    <li><strong>Not reviewing generated types for optional fields</strong> — a generator working from one sample can't know which fields are actually optional across all possible responses.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating generated interfaces as final</strong> without refining names — generated type/property names based on JSON keys may not match your project's naming conventions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Generate Types from JSON</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly typing an API response you're integrating with for the first time</li>
    <li>Bootstrapping types for a JSON config file structure</li>
    <li>Saving time when the alternative is manually writing a large nested interface</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>JSON to TypeScript FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Will this catch every field variation across different API responses?</strong> No — it infers types from the single sample provided; review against your API's actual documented schema for edge cases like optional or nullable fields.</p>
</ToolLayout>
